To run adt-source-cache.sh, save it to a system path (such as /usr/local/bin/adt-source-cache.sh), make it executable, and pass four arguments: package name, version, target install directory, and your build script/command.

1. Install the Wrapper Script

sh
sudo install -m 0755 adt-source-cache.sh /usr/local/bin/adt-source-cache.sh
Use code with caution.

2. Example Usage by Web Server


Nginx (using a dedicated build script)

Create a standalone build script (e.g., /root/scripts/build-nginx.sh) that downloads, configures, compiles, and installs into /usr/local/nginx:
sh
#!/bin/sh
set -eu
VERSION="1.26.2"
cd /tmp
curl -fsSL "https://nginx.org{VERSION}.tar.gz" -o nginx.tar.gz
tar -xzf nginx.tar.gz
cd "nginx-${VERSION}"
./configure --prefix=/usr/local/nginx --with-http_ssl_module --with-http_v2_module
make -j"$(nproc)"
make install
Use code with caution.
Run it through the cache wrapper:
sh
chmod +x /root/scripts/build-nginx.sh
sudo adt-source-cache.sh nginx 1.26.2 /usr/local/nginx /root/scripts/build-nginx.sh
Use code with caution.
• First run (Cache MISS): Compiles Nginx from source, installs to /usr/local/nginx, packages it, and uploads to S3.
• Subsequent runs / new nodes (Cache HIT): Skips /root/scripts/build-nginx.sh completely, downloads the tarball from S3, verifies its SHA-256 checksum, and extracts it to /usr/local/nginx in seconds.

Apache HTTP Server (httpd)

If your build script accepts the version or prefix as arguments, pass them right after the script path:
sh
sudo adt-source-cache.sh apache 2.4.62 /usr/local/apache2 /root/scripts/build-apache.sh 2.4.62
Use code with caution.
(Because 2.4.62 and the contents of build-apache.sh are both hashed into CONFIG_HASH, changing either one automatically triggers a fresh build.)

Lighttpd (using a one-liner shell command)

For simple builds without a separate script file, invoke sh -c:
sh
sudo adt-source-cache.sh lighttpd 1.4.76 /usr/local/lighttpd \
  sh -c 'cd /tmp && curl -fsSL https://lighttpd.net | tar -xJ && cd lighttpd-1.4.76 && ./configure --prefix=/usr/local/lighttpd && make -j$(nproc) && make install'
Use code with caution.

3. Useful Runtime Overrides

You can control behavior at runtime using environment variables:
• Force a fresh compile & overwrite the S3 cache:sh
sudo FORCE_REBUILD=1 adt-source-cache.sh nginx 1.26.2 /usr/local/nginx /root/scripts/build-nginx.sh
Use code with caution.
• Override the target S3 bucket name:sh
sudo SOURCE_CACHE_BUCKET="my-custom-artifacts-bucket" adt-source-cache.sh nginx 1.26.2 /usr/local/nginx /root/scripts/build-nginx.sh
Use code with caution.
• Pass custom compiler flags (automatically creates a separate cache key):sh
sudo CFLAGS="-O3 -march=native" adt-source-cache.sh nginx 1.26.2 /usr/local/nginx /root/scripts/build-nginx.sh
Use code with caution.

Important Reminder on Runtime Libraries

When a node gets a Cache HIT, your build script does not run—so any apt/nala calls inside that build script won't execute either. Always install runtime dependencies (such as libssl3, libpcre2-8-0, zlib1g) in your ADT provisioning step before calling adt-source-cache.sh.
